## 7. Code documentation standards (required for manual edits)

The project owner edits ship sim code by hand. **Every change to ship movement, physics, prediction, or shooting must include clear comments** so files are readable without chat context.

### File-level (`/// <summary>` on class/struct)

Explain in 2–4 sentences:
- What world(s) and system group it runs in.
- Whether it is sim, presentation, or VFX-only.
- What it reads and what it writes.
- Cross-reference paired systems (e.g. client prediction ↔ server authority).

Example pattern:
```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Client-side prediction for the local owner's ship. Runs the same motor step as the server
/// for entities tagged <see cref="Simulate"/> during the predicted fixed-step loop, before
/// Unity Physics integrates position. Paired with <see cref="ShipMovementSystem"/> (server).
/// </summary>
```

### Method-level (non-obvious logic only)

Add `/// <summary>` or `//` blocks when:
- Motor writes velocity but not position (physics contract).
- A query filter matters (`Simulate`, `GhostOwnerIsLocal`, world filter).
- Toroidal map, orbit motor, or shield repel affects behavior.
- Client vs server behavior differs.

### Inline comments (use sparingly but precisely)

Comment **why**, not what:
```csharp
// integratePosition: false — PhysicsSystemGroup owns hull position and bounce contacts.
// InverseInertia zero — collision impulses must not spin the ship; motor owns Rotation.
```

### Section headers in long files

Use `// --- Prediction motor ---` style blocks in `ShipMovementLogic` and similar so manual navigation is easy.

### When adding new systems

Include a short **pipeline comment** at the top listing execution order relative to neighbors:
```csharp
// Order: ShipInputApplySystem → ShipClientPredictedMovementSystem → PhysicsSystemGroup → …
```

### Do not

- Remove existing architecture comments unless they are wrong — update them instead.
- Add noise comments on every line (`// increment i`).
- Leave commented-out old smoothing / motor paths — delete dead code or mark `// LEGACY: remove in migration` with a one-line reason.

